{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"tension-scan","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tension scan"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use when:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" individually sensible demands appear to collide, or new evidence\nmay have changed an earlier conflict."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inputs:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the obligations and their sources, the condition in which they\ninteract, and any earlier formulation. Record which statements are accepted\ncontracts, user goals, current behavior, or your own inferences."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"operation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Operation"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Establish why each demand matters independently. If only one has support,\nreturn that asymmetry rather than manufacturing two equally sound sides."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State the collision concretely: we need A and B, but under C, satisfying A\nundermines B through mechanism M. Work through a legal history or design\nconsequence; a pair of opposing slogans is insufficient."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Look for a missing distinction in scope, authority, ordering, ownership, or\ncheckpoint. Recompute the consequences with that distinction explicit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check whether both original obligations survive. Removing a guarantee can\nbe a proposed tradeoff, but does not demonstrate that the tension dissolved."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On a recheck, identify the evidence that makes the tension remain live,\nsharpen, move, weaken, or dissolve. Read it against the original purpose;\nrecent or vivid material alone does not justify changing the question."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the sourced demands, concrete collision and mechanism, possible\nmissing distinction, and current status; or no supported tension, with the\nmissing support identified. Preserve materially different interpretations when\nthe available authority cannot choose among them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Control:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" restate the case without an A-versus-B framing. Does the conflict\nstill exist? Keep a generated collision separate from an observed one."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Distortion:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" invented symmetry, apparent resolution by deleting an obligation,\nor using an ordinary clarification to claim a major design discovery."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stop when:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the collision or its dissolution is demonstrated at the stated\nscope, or a specific missing fact or design decision is identified."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapted from Field Lab's tension statement, with selection handled by the\nrepository task rather than an interactive instrument menu."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"tension-scan","text":"Tension scan","level":1},{"id":"operation","text":"Operation","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Tension scan\n\n**Use when:** individually sensible demands appear to collide, or new evidence\nmay have changed an earlier conflict.\n\n**Inputs:** the obligations and their sources, the condition in which they\ninteract, and any earlier formulation. Record which statements are accepted\ncontracts, user goals, current behavior, or your own inferences.\n\n## Operation\n\n1. Establish why each demand matters independently. If only one has support,\n   return that asymmetry rather than manufacturing two equally sound sides.\n2. State the collision concretely: we need A and B, but under C, satisfying A\n   undermines B through mechanism M. Work through a legal history or design\n   consequence; a pair of opposing slogans is insufficient.\n3. Look for a missing distinction in scope, authority, ordering, ownership, or\n   checkpoint. Recompute the consequences with that distinction explicit.\n4. Check whether both original obligations survive. Removing a guarantee can\n   be a proposed tradeoff, but does not demonstrate that the tension dissolved.\n5. On a recheck, identify the evidence that makes the tension remain live,\n   sharpen, move, weaken, or dissolve. Read it against the original purpose;\n   recent or vivid material alone does not justify changing the question.\n\n**Return:** the sourced demands, concrete collision and mechanism, possible\nmissing distinction, and current status; or no supported tension, with the\nmissing support identified. Preserve materially different interpretations when\nthe available authority cannot choose among them.\n\n**Control:** restate the case without an A-versus-B framing. Does the conflict\nstill exist? Keep a generated collision separate from an observed one.\n\n**Distortion:** invented symmetry, apparent resolution by deleting an obligation,\nor using an ordinary clarification to claim a major design discovery.\n\n**Stop when:** the collision or its dissolution is demonstrated at the stated\nscope, or a specific missing fact or design decision is identified.\n\nAdapted from Field Lab's tension statement, with selection handled by the\nrepository task rather than an interactive instrument menu.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Tension scan\n\n**Use when:** individually sensible demands appear to collide, or new evidence\nmay have changed an earlier conflict.\n\n**Inputs:** the obligations and their sources, the condition in which they\ninteract, and any earlier formulation. Record which statements are accepted\ncontracts, user goals, current behavior, or your own inferences.\n\n## Operation\n\n1. Establish why each demand matters independently. If only one has support,\n   return that asymmetry rather than manufacturing two equally sound sides.\n2. State the collision concretely: we need A and B, but under C, satisfying A\n   undermines B through mechanism M. Work through a legal history or design\n   consequence; a pair of opposing slogans is insufficient.\n3. Look for a missing distinction in scope, authority, ordering, ownership, or\n   checkpoint. Recompute the consequences with that distinction explicit.\n4. Check whether both original obligations survive. Removing a guarantee can\n   be a proposed tradeoff, but does not demonstrate that the tension dissolved.\n5. On a recheck, identify the evidence that makes the tension remain live,\n   sharpen, move, weaken, or dissolve. Read it against the original purpose;\n   recent or vivid material alone does not justify changing the question.\n\n**Return:** the sourced demands, concrete collision and mechanism, possible\nmissing distinction, and current status; or no supported tension, with the\nmissing support identified. Preserve materially different interpretations when\nthe available authority cannot choose among them.\n\n**Control:** restate the case without an A-versus-B framing. Does the conflict\nstill exist? Keep a generated collision separate from an observed one.\n\n**Distortion:** invented symmetry, apparent resolution by deleting an obligation,\nor using an ordinary clarification to claim a major design discovery.\n\n**Stop when:** the collision or its dissolution is demonstrated at the stated\nscope, or a specific missing fact or design decision is identified.\n\nAdapted from Field Lab's tension statement, with selection handled by the\nrepository task rather than an interactive instrument menu.\n"}
